      "Language": "jpn",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to investigate which dose of fentanyl in ropivacaine for epidural anesthesia will provide effective analgesia with minimal side effects after cesarean section (CS)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Fifty eight patients scheduled for CS were randomly allocated to two groups according to fentanyl dose in epidural analgesia: group F1 (11 microg x hr(-1); n=30) or group F 2 (21 microg x hr(-1); n= 28). Ropivacaine 0.2% 100 ml with fentanyl 400 or 800 microg was administered into the epidural space in the groups F1 and F 2, respectively. Pain scores (visual analogue scale: VAS) with cough or movement, Bromage score, incidence of diclofenac or pentazocine administration, satisfaction score (VAS) and side effects (nausea, vomiting and pruritus) were recorded after CS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Pain scores with cough or movement were significantly lower in the group F 2 than the group F 1 at twelve and twenty-four hours after CS. Bromage score at twelve hours was lower in the group F 2 than the group F 1. The incidences of side effects were similar between the two groups. Satisfaction score was significantly higher in the group F 2 than the group F 1."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "We conclude that continuous epidural administration of fentanyl 21 microg x hr(-1) with ropivacaine provides the optimum balance between pain relief and side effects compared with fentanyl 11 microg x hr(-1) with ropivacaine after CS."
        }
      ]},
